{
  "0": "Scale input item embeddings by a fixed training-frequency feature: w(i)=clip((f(i)/median_positive_frequency)^(-0.15),0.5,2.0), with padding weight zero. Compute counts solely from each category's training interactions and save the weights for identical inference. This may reduce dominance by common history items. Preserve embedding parameters, GRU topology, readout, candidate scoring, loss and optimizer.",
  "1": "Exclude left-padding steps from recurrent computation by packing each observed history, then scatter GRU outputs back to their original positions for aligned next-item supervision. Read inference states at the final real position. This should prevent padding-driven hidden-state drift, especially with median histories of 7–8 interactions. Preserve truncation length, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer; mirror sequence handling in the predictor.",
  "2": "Replace pairwise BPR with equal-weight positive and negative logistic losses: mean[softplus(-positive_score)+softplus(negative_score)] over valid target positions. Use exactly the existing sampled triples and masking. Explicitly penalizing high negative scores may improve ranking stability compared with optimizing score differences alone. Preserve features, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, optimizer, sampling and training schedule.",
  "3": "Apply shared row-wise L2 normalization to the tied item embedding lookups used for GRU inputs and positive, negative and candidate scoring, with epsilon protection and padding fixed at zero. Retain the existing embedding dimension and dot-product scoring. Constraining embedding norms may reduce frequency-driven score inflation and improve generalization. Preserve features, GRU topology, history handling, readout, loss and optimizer; implement identical normalization in inference."
}
